          <div id="faq-under-the-hood-panel" class="faq__panel" hidden>
            <div class="faq__panel-inner">
              <div class="faq__answer">
                <p>
                  Nail Bite Beaver uses built-in functionality on every Mac
                  device called “CoreML”. We first start by taking a picture
                  every second when movement is occurring, or every few seconds
                  if nothing has changed (to save battery). This picture is then
                  fed through a CoreML model that works out what is a hand, and
                  what is a face/mouth, it then works out the distance between
                  them to calculate a confidence score (it uses a combination of
                  the last 3 pictures). Essentially once a picture has been
                  taken, the model is no longer working from the picture, it
                  builds its own virtual hand and mouth with limbs and joints.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Nothing is saved, it all happens really quickly in memory. We
                  keep the last 3 frames in memory to show you if you’ve been
                  caught, but no more than 3 are present at any given time.
                  Thanks to the power of modern Mac devices, nothing is sent
                  over the network, it can all happen locally.
                </p>
                <p>
                  If you’ve got any further questions about the technical
                  details, I’d be more than happy to chat about it, send me an
                  email at{" "}
                  <a href="mailto:support@nailbitebeaver.com"
                    >support@nailbitebeaver.com</a
                  >.
                </p>
